q("U6", "6.3", "Transcription and RNA Processing", "4.B", "A eukaryotic transcript is 2,400 nucleotides before processing and 1,050 nucleotides after processing. Which statement describes the result?", "RNA processing removed 1,350 nucleotides from the primary transcript.", ["RNA processing added 1,350 nucleotides to the coding sequence.", "Translation removed 1,050 nucleotides from the DNA template.", "The mature RNA contains 2,400 nucleotides of joined exons."]);
q("U6", "6.4", "Translation", "1.C", "A mutation changes an mRNA codon from UAU to UAA. What is the most likely effect during translation?", "Translation terminates prematurely at the new stop codon.", ["A tyrosine is inserted and translation continues unchanged.", "The ribosome switches to reading the mRNA in the opposite direction.", "DNA polymerase removes the altered codon before translation."]);
q("U6", "6.6", "Gene Expression and Cell Specialization", "4.B", "Gene X expression is 90 units in muscle, 8 in neurons, and 6 in liver; gene Y expression is 5, 82, and 9 units, respectively. Which pattern is shown?", "Gene X is enriched in muscle, while gene Y is enriched in neurons.", ["Both genes are expressed most strongly in liver.", "Gene X and gene Y have identical expression in all tissues.", "Gene Y expression exceeds gene X expression in muscle."]);

q("U7", "7.1", "Introduction to Natural Selection", "1.B", "Which condition is necessary for natural selection to change a population over generations?", "Individuals differ in heritable traits that affect reproductive success.", ["Individuals acquire the traits they need after environmental change.", "Each genotype produces the same number of surviving offspring.", "Phenotypic variation is caused entirely by nonheritable factors."]);
q("U7", "7.2", "Natural Selection", "6.C", "A drought leaves mostly hard seeds, and birds with deeper beaks produce more surviving offspring. Which prediction follows?", "Alleles associated with deeper beaks may increase in frequency in later generations.", ["Individual birds will deepen their beaks during life and transmit the change.", "Seed hardness will cause the same beak mutation in each offspring.", "Beak-depth variation will disappear before reproductive success differs."]);
q("U7", "7.3", "Artificial Selection", "3.C", "To test the response to artificial selection for larger fruit, breeders should", "select the largest fruits as parents for several replicated lines and compare descendants with unselected control lines", ["compare one large-fruited species with one small-fruited species", "select large fruits while changing fertilizer in each generation", "measure the original population after one generation without breeding it"]);
q("U7", "7.4", "Population Genetics", "5.A", "A population contains 30 AA, 50 Aa, and 20 aa individuals. What is the frequency of allele a?", "0.45", ["0.20", "0.35", "0.70"]);
q("U7", "7.5", "Hardy–Weinberg Equilibrium", "5.A", "In a Hardy–Weinberg population, the recessive phenotype occurs at frequency 0.16. What is the expected frequency of the dominant allele?", "0.60", ["0.16", "0.40", "0.84"]);
q("U7", "7.7", "Common Ancestry", "6.B", "Cytochrome-c sequences from species P and Q differ at 2 amino acid positions, while P and R differ at 18 positions. Which claim is best supported?", "P and Q likely share a more recent common ancestor than P and R.", ["P evolved directly into Q because their sequences are similar.", "R is older than P because its sequence differs at more positions.", "Sequence difference establishes that P and R lack any common ancestor."]);
q("U7", "7.8", "Continuing Evolution", "4.B", "A resistance allele rises from 0.08 to 0.21 to 0.46 across three sampling years after antibiotic use begins. Which trend is shown?", "The resistance allele increases in frequency over time.", ["The allele decreases by a constant amount each year.", "The allele remains at Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium because it is present each year.", "The data show that individual bacteria changed genotype after exposure."]);
q("U7", "7.11", "Variations in Populations", "1.C", "Two plants with the same genotype develop different leaf sizes under high and low light. The difference demonstrates", "environmental influence on phenotypic variation", ["speciation caused by one generation of light exposure", "a change in the genetic code under low light", "independent assortment during somatic cell division"]);
q("U7", "7.12", "Origins of Life on Earth", "6.B", "Which finding would most strongly support a model in which RNA preceded DNA and proteins?", "An RNA molecule can copy a template sequence and catalyze formation of phosphodiester bonds.", ["A protein enzyme can catalyze a reaction in a modern cell.", "A DNA molecule remains stable when purified from a cell.", "A phospholipid bilayer forms spontaneously in water without nucleotides."]);

q("U8", "8.1", "Responses to the Environment", "6.C", "A nocturnal animal shifts its activity period after repeated exposure to light at night. Which explanation is most consistent with a regulated response?", "Light alters signaling that controls the animal's circadian rhythm.", ["The animal changes allele frequencies within its own somatic cells.", "Light supplies the chemical energy used directly for movement.", "The response requires the animal to become a different species."]);
q("U8", "8.2", "Energy Flow Through Ecosystems", "4.B", "An energy pyramid contains 12,000 kJ in producers, 1,400 kJ in primary consumers, and 160 kJ in secondary consumers. Which pattern is shown?", "Available energy decreases at successive trophic levels.", ["Secondary consumers contain more energy than primary consumers.", "Energy transfer from producers to primary consumers is 50%.", "Energy is recycled back to producers without loss between levels."]);
q("U8", "8.3", "Population Ecology", "5.A", "A population grows from 200 to 260 individuals in one year. Using the starting population, what is its per-capita annual growth rate?", "0.30", ["0.23", "0.60", "1.30"]);
q("U8", "8.4", "Effect of Density on Populations", "6.C", "Two populations experience the same drought, but the denser population also has much higher disease transmission. Which claim is best supported?", "Drought is density independent, while disease transmission is density dependent.", ["Both factors are density dependent because both reduce population size.", "Drought is density dependent, while disease transmission is density independent.", "Neither factor can affect carrying capacity or population growth."]);
q("U8", "8.7", "Disruptions in Ecosystems", "3.C", "To test whether an invasive plant reduces native seedling recruitment, investigators should", "compare replicated invaded and uninvaded plots while controlling other habitat differences", ["compare one invaded plot this year with a different habitat last year", "remove native seedlings only from the uninvaded plots", "measure adult invasive plants without counting native seedlings"]);

q("U1", "1.7", "Proteins", "2.A", "At 55°C, approximately what fraction of each enzyme remains active?", "About 0.30 for the reference and 0.62 for the variant", ["About 0.62 for the reference and 0.30 for the variant", "About 0.95 for both enzymes", "About 0.05 for the reference and 0.22 for the variant"], "apbio-g-u1-protein-stability", BIO_STIMULI.u1ProteinStability);
q("U1", "1.7", "Proteins", "2.C", "Which comparison is best supported by the graph?", "The variant retains activity at higher temperatures better than the reference enzyme.", ["The reference enzyme is more active than the variant at each temperature.", "Both enzymes gain activity as temperature increases above 35°C.", "The variant and reference have different activity at 25°C but converge at 65°C."], "apbio-g-u1-protein-stability", BIO_STIMULI.u1ProteinStability);
q("U1", "1.7", "Proteins", "2.D", "A cell expressing the variant replaces the reference enzyme. Which prediction follows from the model?", "The cell may preserve more enzyme function during a moderate heat stress.", ["The cell will synthesize more enzyme because the graph measures transcription.", "The enzyme will catalyze a different reaction because stability changes substrate identity.", "The cell will lose enzyme function at lower temperatures than the reference cell."], "apbio-g-u1-protein-stability", BIO_STIMULI.u1ProteinStability);
q("U1", "1.7", "Proteins", "2.B", "Which molecular change could account for the pattern shown?", "Additional stabilizing interactions help the variant maintain its folded shape at high temperature.", ["The variant lacks peptide bonds and therefore cannot unfold.", "The reference enzyme gains covalent cross-links as temperature rises.", "The variant uses RNA nucleotides in place of amino acids."], "apbio-g-u1-protein-stability", BIO_STIMULI.u1ProteinStability);

q("U2", "2.2", "Cell Size", "2.A", "Which model cell has the greatest membrane surface per unit cytoplasm?", "The 1 μm cell", ["The 2 μm cell", "The 4 μm cell", "All three have the same surface per unit volume"], "apbio-g-u2-cell-geometry", BIO_STIMULI.u2CellGeometry);
q("U2", "2.2", "Cell Size", "2.C", "What relationship is shown as side length increases?", "Surface-area-to-volume ratio decreases.", ["Surface-area-to-volume ratio increases.", "Volume decreases while surface area remains constant.", "Surface area and volume increase in the same proportion."], "apbio-g-u2-cell-geometry", BIO_STIMULI.u2CellGeometry);
q("U2", "2.2", "Cell Size", "2.D", "If exchange across the membrane limits metabolism, which cell would reach that limit first as demand rises?", "The 4 μm cell, because it has the least surface area per unit volume", ["The 1 μm cell, because it has the greatest surface area per unit volume", "The 2 μm cell, because a 3:1 ratio prevents diffusion", "All three at the same demand because shape is identical"], "apbio-g-u2-cell-geometry", BIO_STIMULI.u2CellGeometry);
q("U2", "2.2", "Cell Size", "2.B", "A 4 μm cell divides into eight 2 μm cells with the same total volume. What change is expected?", "The combined surface area increases, improving exchange capacity.", ["The combined surface area decreases because each daughter is smaller.", "The surface-area-to-volume ratio remains 1.5:1 for the daughters.", "The total volume doubles because eight daughters are produced."], "apbio-g-u2-cell-geometry", BIO_STIMULI.u2CellGeometry);

q("U5", "5.2", "Meiosis and Genetic Diversity", "2.A", "At which modeled stage is genetic material exchanged?", "During pairing, before homologs separate in meiosis I", ["After sister chromatids separate in meiosis II", "After four haploid products have formed", "During DNA replication after meiosis I"], "apbio-g-u5-meiosis", BIO_STIMULI.u5Meiosis);
q("U5", "5.2", "Meiosis and Genetic Diversity", "2.B", "Which chromatids exchange segments in the model?", "Nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes", ["Sister chromatids of one replicated chromosome", "Chromatids from nonhomologous chromosome pairs", "Two unreplicated chromosomes after meiosis II"], "apbio-g-u5-meiosis", BIO_STIMULI.u5Meiosis);
q("U5", "5.1", "Meiosis", "2.C", "Which event shown accounts for reduction from diploid to haploid?", "Homologous chromosomes move into different cells after meiosis I.", ["DNA replicates before homologs pair.", "Sister chromatids move apart after meiosis II.", "Nonsister chromatids exchange corresponding segments."], "apbio-g-u5-meiosis", BIO_STIMULI.u5Meiosis);
q("U5", "5.2", "Meiosis and Genetic Diversity", "2.D", "What is the most direct genetic consequence of the exchange shown?", "Some gametes contain new combinations of alleles on the same chromosome.", ["Each gamete receives both homologs of the chromosome pair.", "Chromosome number increases in all four products.", "The exchanged genes begin assorting during mitosis rather than meiosis."], "apbio-g-u5-meiosis", BIO_STIMULI.u5Meiosis);

q("U7", "7.4", "Population Genetics", "2.A", "What is the approximate frequency of allele R in the treated population at generation 8?", "0.64", ["0.08", "0.36", "0.82"], "apbio-g-u7-selection", BIO_STIMULI.u7Selection);
q("U7", "7.2", "Natural Selection", "2.C", "Which comparison is shown by the two populations?", "Allele R increases under pesticide treatment but remains nearly stable in the control.", ["Allele R increases at the same rate in both populations.", "Allele R declines under pesticide treatment and rises in the control.", "Both populations reach fixation for allele R by generation 10."], "apbio-g-u7-selection", BIO_STIMULI.u7Selection);
q("U7", "7.2", "Natural Selection", "2.D", "If R confers pesticide resistance, which mechanism best accounts for the divergence?", "R carriers leave more surviving offspring in the treated population.", ["Pesticide converts susceptible alleles into R in each exposed insect.", "Control insects stop reproducing after generation 2.", "R prevents inheritance in the untreated population."], "apbio-g-u7-selection", BIO_STIMULI.u7Selection);
q("U7", "7.8", "Continuing Evolution", "2.D", "Pesticide use stops at generation 10, and R reduces fitness without pesticide. What change does the model support predicting?", "R may decrease in later treated-population generations once its advantage is removed.", ["R must remain fixed because allele frequencies cannot reverse.", "R will immediately disappear from each resistant insect.", "The control population will acquire R at the treated population's final frequency."], "apbio-g-u7-selection", BIO_STIMULI.u7Selection);

const BIO_EXPLANATIONS = {
  "apbio-u1-001": "Evaporation preferentially removes faster-moving water molecules, and breaking their hydrogen-bond interactions requires energy. The remaining water therefore has lower average kinetic energy; covalent O–H bonds are not broken during evaporation.",
  "apbio-u1-002": "Rubisco adds carbon dioxide to RuBP, producing an unstable six-carbon intermediate that immediately splits into 3-phosphoglycerate. Oxygen comes from water splitting, and ATP contains no newly fixed carbon.",
  "apbio-u1-003": "Dehydration synthesis removes water while forming a covalent bond between monomers. Hydrolysis does the reverse, whereas redox reactions and diffusion do not build a polymer backbone.",
  "apbio-u1-004": "Starch primarily uses alpha glycosidic linkages, while cellulose uses beta-1,4 linkages. That orientation changes chain shape, enzyme recognition, and function even though both polymers contain glucose.",
  "apbio-u1-005": "Shorter tails have fewer dispersion interactions, and cis double bonds introduce packing-disrupting bends. Both changes increase fluidity rather than strengthening tail packing.",
  "apbio-u1-006": "RNA contains ribose with a hydroxyl group at the 2-prime carbon; DNA contains deoxyribose with hydrogen there. Both nucleotide types can contain phosphate, bases, and phosphodiester bonds.",
  "apbio-u1-007": "A charged surface residue can form ionic or hydrogen-bond interactions with water and other residues. Replacing it with a nonpolar side chain can alter folding without changing peptide-bond type or the mRNA reading frame.",
  "apbio-u1-008": "Water's polar covalent bonds create partial charges that support hydrogen bonding with other water molecules and polar vessel walls. Those attractions produce cohesion and adhesion.",
  "apbio-u1-009": "Triacylglycerols are highly reduced and stored without a large hydration shell, so they provide more energy per unit mass than glycogen. Glycogen remains readily hydrolyzed and feeds glucose into glycolysis.",
  "apbio-u1-010": "If activity stays low after the enzyme is returned to its original temperature, heating caused a persistent structural change rather than a temporary change in collision rate. Adding substrate at 90°C does not test recovery after cooling.",
  "apbio-u1-011": "Complete hydrolysis of P yields only glucose, which identifies it as a glucose polymer such as a polysaccharide. Proteins yield amino acids, and nucleic acids yield sugar, phosphate, and bases.",
  "apbio-u1-012": "Amino acids are protein monomers, and many contain nitrogen in amino groups or side chains. The alternative product patterns describe carbohydrates, nucleic acids, or lipids rather than proteins.",
  "apbio-u1-013": "Pentose sugar, phosphate, and nitrogenous bases are nucleotide components. Nucleotides are covalently joined through phosphodiester bonds in the sugar-phosphate backbone, not peptide or glycosidic bonds.",
  "apbio-u1-014": "A reagent-only tube follows the same procedure without polymer, so detected products would reveal reagent contamination. Changing polymer mass or temperature introduces another variable and does not isolate contamination.",
  "apbio-u1-015": "Q is a protein and R is a nucleic acid, yet both yield nitrogen-containing components. The data therefore support nitrogen in more than one macromolecule class, not a universal link between nitrogen and phosphorus.",
  "apbio-u1-016": "The label is already present in 3-phosphoglycerate at 10 seconds but is absent from glucose until later. That temporal pattern places 3-phosphoglycerate earlier in the path from carbon dioxide to carbohydrate.",
  "apbio-u1-017": "Branch points create many nonreducing ends where enzymes can add or remove glucose rapidly. Disabling the branching enzyme produces less-branched glycogen without changing glucose into another monomer or linkage class.",
  "apbio-u1-018": "Chargaff's rules give A = T = 28%, leaving 44% for G plus C. Because G equals C, guanine accounts for 22% of the nucleotides.",
  "apbio-u1-019": "A targeted charged-to-uncharged substitution tests whether the proposed salt bridge contributes to stability while preserving the rest of the design. Changing temperature, concentration, or a distant residue does not isolate that interaction.",
  "apbio-u1-020": "Unsaturated fatty acids decline from 68% to 52% to 37% as temperature rises. The data show a negative relationship, consistent with organisms using more unsaturation to maintain fluidity at low temperature.",

  "apbio-u2-001": "Secreted proteins are synthesized on rough-ER ribosomes and then modified, sorted, and packaged by the Golgi apparatus. Chloroplasts, central vacuoles, and cell walls are not features of pancreatic cells.",
  "apbio-u2-002": "For a sphere, surface-area-to-volume ratio equals 3 divided by radius. Doubling radius from 1 to 2 micrometers therefore halves the ratio rather than doubling or preserving it.",
  "apbio-u2-003": "Hydrophilic phosphate heads interact with water, while hydrophobic fatty-acid tails minimize contact with it by clustering inward. The arrangement is driven by noncovalent interactions, not ionization or covalent bonding to water.",
  "apbio-u2-004": "A proton leak dissipates the electrochemical gradient that powers ATP synthase, directly reducing chemiosmotic ATP production. Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol and does not depend on the mitochondrial proton gradient.",
  "apbio-u2-005": "Oxygen is small and nonpolar, so it crosses the lipid bilayer down its concentration gradient without ATP or a transport protein. Pumps and endocytosis require cellular machinery and energy.",
  "apbio-u2-006": "Removing glucose carriers reduces facilitated diffusion of glucose. It does not stop osmosis, create a phagocytic route for glucose, or drive glucose against its gradient.",
  "apbio-u2-007": "The cell's water potential is −0.7 + 0.3 = −0.4 MPa. Water initially moves from the solution at −0.2 MPa to the cell at −0.4 MPa, from higher to lower water potential.",
  "apbio-u2-008": "ATP phosphorylation changes the pump's conformation and affinity for sodium and potassium, allowing both ions to move against their gradients. Diffusion through lipid or vesicle engulfment would not perform this selective exchange.",
  "apbio-u2-009": "Lysosomes isolate acid hydrolases from cytoplasmic substrates. Leakage would expose proteins, nucleic acids, and organelles to degradation; it would not accelerate photosynthesis or oxidative phosphorylation.",
  "apbio-u2-010": "Circular DNA and division resembling binary fission are bacterial-like features retained by mitochondria. Protein import and replication during the cell cycle are compatible with endosymbiosis but are less specific evidence.",
  "apbio-u2-011": "X crosses the phospholipid region without a protein, the expected route for a small nonpolar molecule. Y requires a channel and Z requires an ATP-coupled pump, indicating greater polarity or charge.",
  "apbio-u2-012": "Y moves through a channel from higher to lower concentration without ATP, which is facilitated diffusion. Simple diffusion would not use the channel, and active transport would move against a gradient using energy.",
  "apbio-u2-013": "Z is moved from the lower-concentration exterior to the higher-concentration cytosol, so the pump must couple transport to ATP. X and Y move downhill and can continue without ATP while their gradients persist.",
  "apbio-u2-014": "Comparing transport of Y with a similar solute through the same channel directly tests molecular selectivity. The other designs either lack a comparison solute or change multiple variables.",
  "apbio-u2-015": "An open passive pore lets Z move down its electrochemical gradient until the concentrations approach equilibrium. A passive pore cannot maintain or steepen the gradient through ATP coupling.",
  "apbio-u2-016": "Golgi tags help direct proteins to secretion, lysosomes, membranes, or other destinations. Transcription, glycolytic ATP production, and chromosome replication occur through different cellular machinery.",
  "apbio-u2-017": "A 2-micrometer cube has surface area 24 and volume 8, a ratio of 3:1. A 4-micrometer cube has surface area 96 and volume 64, a ratio of 1.5:1.",
  "apbio-u2-018": "Water moves from the cell at −0.6 MPa to the solution at −0.9 MPa because −0.6 is the higher water potential. The fact that both values are negative does not imply equilibrium.",
  "apbio-u2-019": "A fraction holding only 8% of protein but 74% of catalase activity is enriched for catalase and likely contains catalase-rich organelles. Uniform distribution would make the two percentages more similar.",
  "apbio-u2-020": "The organelle and bacterial ribosomes share a similar sedimentation behavior that differs from cytosolic eukaryotic ribosomes. That pattern is consistent with an organelle derived from a bacterial ancestor.",

  "apbio-u3-001": "Enzymes stabilize a transition state and lower activation energy, increasing reaction rate without changing the reaction's overall free-energy change or equilibrium. They are regenerated rather than consumed as reactants.",
  "apbio-u3-002": "Induced fit means substrate binding changes the enzyme's conformation to position catalytic groups favorably. It does not alter primary sequence or change an endergonic reaction's thermodynamic sign.",
  "apbio-u3-003": "Extreme pH changes protonation of amino-acid side chains, disrupting ionic and hydrogen-bond interactions that shape the active site. Low pH does not necessarily hydrolyze the backbone or lower substrate concentration.",
  "apbio-u3-004": "Substrate concentration must remain constant so temperature is the only manipulated factor affecting catalase rate. Temperature is the independent variable and oxygen-production rate is the dependent variable.",
  "apbio-u3-005": "Coupling adds the free-energy changes of the reactions. If ATP hydrolysis releases more free energy than the endergonic reaction requires, the combined process has a negative delta G and can proceed.",
  "apbio-u3-006": "The uncoupler lets proton-motive energy dissipate as heat instead of passing through ATP synthase. Electron transport and oxygen consumption may continue or rise, but ATP yield falls.",
  "apbio-u3-007": "Photosystem II oxidizes water, releasing electrons, protons, and molecular oxygen. Carbon dioxide supplies carbon for the Calvin cycle but is not the source of released oxygen.",
  "apbio-u3-008": "Blocking photosystem II deprives the electron-transport chain of electrons, reducing NADP-plus reduction and therefore NADPH production. The other choices describe respiration or fermentation.",
  "apbio-u3-009": "Fermentation oxidizes NADH back to NAD-plus, allowing glycolysis to continue producing ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation. It does not outproduce oxidative phosphorylation.",
  "apbio-u3-010": "The electron-transport chain establishes a proton gradient, and proton flow through ATP synthase drives most ATP production. Glycolysis produces only a small fraction by substrate-level phosphorylation.",
  "apbio-u3-011": "The no-inhibitor rate changes only from about 31 to 32 as substrate rises from 5 to 10 mM, showing a plateau as active sites become occupied. Neither enzyme amount nor reaction direction changes in the graph.",
  "apbio-u3-012": "At 2.0 mM, the rate falls from 22 to 15; the reduction is 7 divided by 22, or about 0.318. Rounded to the nearest percent, the decrease is 32%.",
  "apbio-u3-013": "Inhibitor A strongly lowers rate at low substrate but approaches the uninhibited rate at high substrate. That pattern is consistent with substrate competing away much of A's effect.",
  "apbio-u3-014": "With B, rate plateaus near 16 rather than the uninhibited maximum near 32, even as substrate rises. The data therefore do not show recovery of the original maximum at high substrate.",
  "apbio-u3-015": "Separate preincubations followed by removal of free B reveal whether a lasting change follows exposure of enzyme or substrate. Merely changing several concentrations or pH values would confound the interpretation.",
  "apbio-u3-016": "Putting substrate concentration on the x-axis and rate on the y-axis displays how the response changes and whether it approaches a plateau. Pie charts and unlabeled categorical plots cannot show that functional relationship.",
  "apbio-u3-017": "Cooling the same enzyme back to the original assay conditions tests recovery directly. If activity returns, inhibition was reversible; changing substrate, pH, or enzyme identity would add confounding variables.",
  "apbio-u3-018": "ATP falls by 2.7 mM while product rises by 2.5 mM, so product formation is associated with ATP consumption. The data alone support coupling, not direct conversion of product into ATP.",
  "apbio-u3-019": "Chlorophyll reflects much of the green light and absorbs red and blue wavelengths more strongly. Green-only illumination therefore supplies less usable excitation energy without directly disabling rubisco or water entry.",
  "apbio-u3-020": "Oxygen consumption rises from 18 to 42 after ADP and remains about 43 with the stated inhibitor. The description follows the values; it does not claim the inhibitor behaved as ordinarily predicted.",

  "apbio-u4-001": "Yeast mating factor acts over a short distance on nearby target cells, making it local signaling. Endocrine signals travel through circulation, which yeast lack.",
  "apbio-u4-002": "Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble and can diffuse through the phospholipid bilayer to intracellular receptors. They do not require ion channels or signal peptides to cross the plasma membrane.",
  "apbio-u4-003": "GTP hydrolysis normally returns the G protein to its inactive GDP-bound state. Preventing hydrolysis prolongs effector activation after the receptor signal begins.",
  "apbio-u4-004": "Sweating promotes heat loss and moves elevated temperature back toward its set point, opposing the original deviation. Amplifying the increase would be positive feedback.",
  "apbio-u4-005": "During anaphase, cohesin release permits sister chromatids to move toward opposite poles. DNA replication occurs in S phase, before mitosis.",
  "apbio-u4-006": "p53 can stop the cycle for repair or trigger apoptosis after serious damage. Without that safeguard, mutation-bearing cells may continue dividing and contribute to a tumor.",
  "apbio-u4-007": "Stronger contractions cause more oxytocin release, which strengthens contractions further. Because the response reinforces rather than reverses the stimulus, the loop is positive feedback.",
  "apbio-u4-008": "The intervals are 16–20% and 24–28%, so they do not overlap. That separation supports a higher mitotic index in treated cultures, though it does not imply every treated cell entered mitosis.",
  "apbio-u4-009": "A vehicle-only culture experiences the same handling and solvent without the growth factor, isolating the factor's effect. Changing temperature or density would create confounds.",
  "apbio-u4-010": "One activated molecule can activate many downstream molecules at each step, multiplying the response. Amplification changes signal magnitude; it does not create chromosomes or matter.",
  "apbio-u4-011": "The membrane receptor is the first depicted cellular component to bind the extracellular ligand. Relay proteins A and B receive information only after receptor activation.",
  "apbio-u4-012": "One active B generating many C molecules is a direct visual example of amplification. It increases signal magnitude rather than terminating or reversing the pathway.",
  "apbio-u4-013": "The model places A upstream of B, so inactive A fails to activate B and little second messenger C is produced. Neither the receptor nor phosphatase bypasses that missing relay in the diagram.",
  "apbio-u4-014": "The phosphatase removes activating phosphate groups from relay proteins. Inhibiting it slows pathway shutoff, so signaling can persist after ligand removal.",
  "apbio-u4-015": "Comparing otherwise matched cells with and without the receptor gene isolates receptor necessity. Changing dose and temperature together or removing a downstream protein cannot specifically test ligand reception.",
  "apbio-u4-016": "A neurotransmitter crossing a short synaptic gap acts locally. Endocrine signaling requires long-distance transport, usually through circulatory fluid.",
  "apbio-u4-017": "Response rises sharply from 0 to 68 units, then changes only to 70 at the highest dose. That plateau is consistent with saturation of receptors or downstream components.",
  "apbio-u4-018": "Temperature declines from 39.0 toward 37.1 degrees, showing correction of the perturbation toward a set point. Moving farther away would indicate amplification rather than negative feedback.",
  "apbio-u4-019": "Dividing 65 mitotic cells by 500 total cells gives 0.13, or 13%. The much larger interphase count is consistent with cells spending more time in interphase.",
  "apbio-u4-020": "Matched damaged cells differing only in compound exposure isolate the compound's effect on checkpoint arrest. Designs that alter species, damage, nutrients, or timing cannot support the same causal conclusion.",

  "apbio-u5-001": "The first meiotic division separates homologous chromosome pairs, reducing each cell from two homologs to one. Sister chromatids remain joined until meiosis II.",
  "apbio-u5-002": "If homologs fail to separate in meiosis I, both move to one pole. After meiosis II, gametes receive either two copies or no copy of that chromosome rather than the normal one.",
  "apbio-u5-003": "Crossing over exchanges corresponding DNA between nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, creating new allele combinations. It does not delete whole chromosomes or prevent homolog pairing.",
  "apbio-u5-004": "Independent assortment gives 2 to the n possible maternal/paternal chromosome combinations. With n = 4, the number is 2 to the fourth power, or 16.",
  "apbio-u5-005": "Aa crossed with Aa produces AA, Aa, Aa, and aa. Only aa has the recessive phenotype under complete dominance, giving a probability of one fourth.",
  "apbio-u5-006": "A homozygous recessive tester contributes only recessive alleles, so offspring phenotypes reveal which allele came from the unknown parent. Dominant or heterozygous testers obscure that inference.",
  "apbio-u5-007": "A heterozygote with an intermediate pink phenotype is the defining pattern of incomplete dominance. Codominance would display both parental phenotypes distinctly.",
  "apbio-u5-008": "Each son receives Y from his father and one maternal X. A heterozygous mother transmits the recessive allele to half of her sons on average.",
  "apbio-u5-009": "Using genetically similar animals and changing only temperature isolates the environmental variable. Comparing unrelated species or changing diet simultaneously confounds genotype and environment.",
  "apbio-u5-010": "Height is polygenic and responsive to nutrition, health, and other environmental conditions. Shared alleles therefore do not require identical adult phenotypes.",
  "apbio-u5-011": "The two largest classes, long-red and short-brown, retain the parental allele combinations. The smaller classes are recombinants produced by crossing over.",
  "apbio-u5-012": "The recombinant classes total 94 + 96 = 190 of 1,000 offspring. Dividing 190 by 1,000 gives a recombination frequency of 0.19, or 19%.",
  "apbio-u5-013": "Two large parental classes and two smaller recombinant classes are the expected pattern for linked genes with occasional crossing over. Independent assortment would approach equal class sizes.",
  "apbio-u5-014": "Greater physical distance generally provides more opportunity for a crossover between loci, increasing recombinant frequency until it approaches 50%. It does not stop homolog separation.",
  "apbio-u5-015": "Because 385 is far greater than the 7.815 critical value, the deviation from equal expected classes is unlikely under independent assortment. The null hypothesis should be rejected, not accepted as proven.",
  "apbio-u5-016": "After meiosis I, chromosome number is reduced because homologs have separated, but each chromosome still contains joined sister chromatids. Sister separation during meiosis II does not further reduce chromosome number.",
  "apbio-u5-017": "Map distance in centimorgans approximates recombinant percentage for moderately close loci. Twelve map units therefore predicts about 12% recombinant offspring in an informative testcross.",
  "apbio-u5-018": "A Punnett square explicitly combines parental gametes to predict offspring genotype frequencies. Food webs, age plots, and cladograms represent unrelated biological relationships.",
  "apbio-u5-019": "A population may contain many alleles at one locus, but a diploid individual has only two homologous copies and therefore at most two alleles. Codominance affects phenotype, not segregation or allele number per individual.",
  "apbio-u5-020": "Replicating each genotype across each environment allows genotype, environment, and their interaction to be compared. Assigning different genotypes to different environments confounds those effects.",

  "apbio-u6-001": "Antiparallel strands have opposite backbone orientations, so one runs 5-prime to 3-prime while its complement runs 3-prime to 5-prime. Both strands contain deoxyribose and nucleotides.",
  "apbio-u6-002": "DNA polymerase reads a template and adds each nucleotide to the free 3-prime hydroxyl of the growing strand. It does not synthesize protein or extend a strand without a template.",
  "apbio-u6-003": "Splicing removes introns and joins exons in eukaryotic pre-mRNA. Translation occurs after export, and RNA retains uracil rather than replacing it with thymine.",
  "apbio-u6-004": "The tRNA anticodon base-pairs antiparallel with a complementary mRNA codon in the ribosome. Promoters are DNA sites used during transcription, not translation.",
  "apbio-u6-005": "Enhancer-bound transcription factors interact with mediator and promoter-associated proteins to change transcription initiation. They do not alter coding sequence, chromosome number, or membrane transport.",
  "apbio-u6-006": "Most somatic cells share a genome, but different regulatory programs activate different gene sets. Those expression differences produce the proteins and functions characteristic of specialized cells.",
  "apbio-u6-007": "A one-nucleotide insertion changes how downstream nucleotides are grouped into codons, often altering many amino acids and introducing an early stop. A substitution would more often affect only one codon.",
  "apbio-u6-008": "PCR uses primers and repeated denaturation, annealing, and extension cycles to amplify a chosen DNA region. Protein separation and chromosome editing require different methods.",
  "apbio-u6-009": "DNA fragments have similar charge-to-mass ratios, so the gel matrix separates mainly by size; smaller fragments move through pores more readily. They are not converted to RNA and are not more positively charged.",
  "apbio-u6-010": "The genetic code is redundant, so different codons can specify the same amino acid. Such a synonymous substitution can leave the protein sequence unchanged.",
  "apbio-u6-011": "In condition one, the repressor is drawn on the operator next to the promoter, obstructing productive RNA-polymerase transcription. The genes remain in the chromosome and are not separate transcripts in the model.",
  "apbio-u6-012": "If the small molecule cannot alter the repressor, the repressor retains operator affinity in condition two. It therefore continues blocking transcription despite the molecule's presence.",
  "apbio-u6-013": "Deleting the operator removes the repressor-binding site while preserving the promoter, so RNA polymerase can initiate regardless of the regulator. That produces constitutive transcription.",
  "apbio-u6-014": "mRNA abundance measures transcriptional output more directly than final protein or metabolite abundance, which also depends on translation and degradation. Comparing mRNA between conditions isolates the proposed level.",
  "apbio-u6-015": "An operon's structural genes share regulatory DNA and are copied into one polycistronic transcript. They can still contain separate coding regions that produce different proteins.",
  "apbio-u6-016": "A stable helix pairs one purine with one pyrimidine at consistent width, and complementary hydrogen-bonding groups favor G with C. The strands remain antiparallel and are held together noncovalently.",
  "apbio-u6-017": "Ligase seals phosphodiester-bond gaps between adjacent Okazaki fragments. Without it, lagging-strand fragments are synthesized but remain unjoined.",
  "apbio-u6-018": "Subtracting mature length from primary-transcript length gives 2,400 − 1,050 = 1,350 nucleotides removed. This is consistent with intron removal during RNA processing.",
  "apbio-u6-019": "UAA is a stop codon, whereas UAU encodes tyrosine. The substitution creates a nonsense mutation that terminates translation prematurely.",
  "apbio-u6-020": "Gene X is far higher in muscle than the other tissues, while gene Y is far higher in neurons. The reciprocal pattern illustrates tissue-specific gene expression.",

  "apbio-u7-001": "Selection acts on phenotypic differences that affect survival or reproduction, provided those differences are heritable. It does not act on traits produced only because an organism needs them.",
  "apbio-u7-002": "Resistant insects survived pesticide exposure and contributed proportionally more offspring, increasing resistance alleles. The pesticide selected existing heritable variation rather than inducing the same directed mutation.",
  "apbio-u7-003": "Artificial selection differs in the selecting agent: humans choose breeders for desired traits. Mutation, recombination, and heritable variation still supply the differences selection acts on.",
  "apbio-u7-004": "There are 60 A alleles in heterozygotes and 40 in 20 AA homozygotes, for 100 A alleles among 200 total alleles. The frequency is therefore 100 divided by 200, or 0.50.",
  "apbio-u7-005": "If q-squared is 0.09, q is 0.30 and p is 0.70. Heterozygote frequency is 2pq = 2 × 0.70 × 0.30 = 0.42.",
  "apbio-u7-006": "The shared underlying bone pattern is homologous and is best explained by inheritance from a common ancestor followed by modification. Similar ecological function alone would more often produce analogous structures.",
  "apbio-u7-007": "A nearly universal genetic code is a complex shared molecular feature across all domains of life, strongly supporting common ancestry. Habitat or genome size patterns are less universal and less specific.",
  "apbio-u7-008": "Rapid replication creates many generations, and mutation supplies heritable variants on which selection acts. Mutation is not directed toward antibodies, and selection continues during infection.",
  "apbio-u7-009": "Heritable shell-color variants may have different survival or reproductive success under changed conditions, allowing allele frequencies to shift. Nonheritable or need-directed changes would not support evolutionary response in the same way.",
  "apbio-u7-010": "A geographic barrier reduces gene flow, allowing mutation, drift, and selection to produce divergence between populations. Random mating within one panmictic population does not initiate allopatric separation.",
  "apbio-u7-011": "D and E join at the rightmost, most recent node before either joins C. Their spatial proximity alone is not decisive; the shared node is.",
  "apbio-u7-012": "Removing E does not change the existing branching order: D still joins C after their lineage diverges from B. Pruning a taxon does not turn one remaining species into another's direct ancestor.",
  "apbio-u7-013": "Character 3 arises on the branch leading to C, D, and E, while character 4 arises later on the D–E branch. A species with 3 but not 4 belongs after B diverges but outside the D–E clade.",
  "apbio-u7-014": "A cladogram represents inferred ancestry and character distribution, not current reproductive compatibility. The branching relationships among A through E are directly represented, but fertile interbreeding would require separate evidence.",
  "apbio-u7-015": "RNA can carry sequence information and some RNAs catalyze reactions, so demonstrating both functions addresses a central RNA-world prediction. Modern DNA diffusion or protein uniformity would not test self-replicating RNA.",
  "apbio-u7-016": "Selection requires heritable variation linked to differential reproductive success. If all genotypes reproduce equally or variation is entirely nonheritable, allele frequencies will not change by selection.",
  "apbio-u7-017": "Deeper-beaked birds leave more surviving offspring under hard-seed conditions, so associated alleles can become more common. Individuals do not adapt by deliberately changing and transmitting their beaks.",
  "apbio-u7-018": "Replicated selected lines compared with unselected controls isolate the effect of choosing large-fruited parents across generations. Changing fertilizer or omitting breeding prevents a clean test of selection response.",
  "apbio-u7-019": "The population contains 50 a alleles from Aa plus 40 from aa, or 90 of 200 total alleles. The a frequency is 90 divided by 200 = 0.45.",
  "apbio-u7-020": "The recessive phenotype gives q-squared = 0.16, so q = 0.40. Therefore p, the dominant-allele frequency, is 1 − 0.40 = 0.60.",
  "apbio-u7-021": "Fewer sequence differences generally indicate less time since divergence, so P and Q likely share a more recent common ancestor. Sequence similarity does not establish direct ancestry or absolute species age.",
  "apbio-u7-022": "The resistance allele rises from 0.08 to 0.21 to 0.46, a consistent increase across samples. The trend describes population-level frequency change, not genotype change within individual bacteria.",
  "apbio-u7-023": "Because genotype is held constant, the different leaf sizes demonstrate phenotypic plasticity caused by light conditions. One environmental response does not establish speciation or change the genetic code.",
  "apbio-u7-024": "An RNA capable of template copying and catalyzing phosphodiester-bond formation combines information storage with catalytic function, as the model predicts. Modern protein catalysis or DNA stability is less direct evidence.",

  "apbio-u8-001": "Differential elongation in response to directional light is a regulated growth response to an environmental cue. It occurs within one plant and is not population-level drift or interspecific competition.",
  "apbio-u8-002": "Ten percent of 20,000 kJ reaches primary consumers, and ten percent of that reaches secondary consumers: 20,000 × 0.1 × 0.1 = 200 kJ.",
  "apbio-u8-003": "A two-line time-series plot preserves temporal order and lets both populations be compared at each time. Pie charts and histograms do not appropriately represent repeated measurements over time.",
  "apbio-u8-004": "Disease transmission generally increases as individuals become more crowded, making it density dependent. A freeze, drought, or volcanic eruption can affect populations regardless of their density.",
  "apbio-u8-005": "The smaller realized niche in the competitor's presence shows that one species restricts the other's use of shared space. That is interspecific competition rather than mutualism or succession.",
  "apbio-u8-006": "Genetic variation increases the chance that some individuals possess alleles suited to new conditions and reproduce. Diversity does not eliminate competition, mutation, or environmental change.",
  "apbio-u8-007": "A keystone predator can regulate dominant prey, so its removal may trigger trophic cascades and broad shifts in community abundance. The effect is not confined to the missing predator.",
  "apbio-u8-008": "Metabolic transformations release usable energy as heat, which is not returned to producers as chemical energy. Matter can cycle, but energy requires continuing external input.",
  "apbio-u8-009": "Attack frequency can be measured and compared between mimic-colored and nonmimetic individuals, making the question testable. Value judgments and purpose-driven evolutionary claims are not framed as measurable hypotheses.",
  "apbio-u8-010": "Replicated ponds differing only in fertilizer concentration isolate runoff as the independent variable and provide an untreated comparison. Changing several factors or omitting controls prevents causal inference.",
  "apbio-u8-011": "The control curve changes very little between days 8 and 10 near 400 organisms, indicating a plateau near carrying capacity. Earlier intervals show rapid growth rather than equilibrium.",
  "apbio-u8-012": "Control abundance rises from 20 to 62, a gain of 42 in two days. Dividing 42 by the starting 20 and by 2 days gives 1.05 per day.",
  "apbio-u8-013": "Predator cultures level near 130 while controls level near 400, with separated confidence intervals late in the study. The pattern supports a lower equilibrium abundance under predation, not extinction or no effect.",
  "apbio-u8-014": "Varying initial predator density while holding nutrients and starting protist density constant isolates predator-density effects. Changing multiple variables or removing replication would weaken the comparison.",
  "apbio-u8-015": "Heritable behavioral variation gives selection something to act on, so some genotypes may survive predation and reproduce. Variation does not remove predators or guarantee equal survival.",
  "apbio-u8-016": "Light is an environmental input to circadian signaling pathways and can shift regulated activity timing. The behavioral response does not require somatic allele-frequency change or speciation.",
  "apbio-u8-017": "Energy declines from 12,000 to 1,400 to 160 kJ at successive levels, showing loss of available energy during transfer. The producer-to-primary transfer is about 12%, not 50%.",
  "apbio-u8-018": "The population gains 60 individuals from a starting 200. Per-capita growth is 60 divided by 200 = 0.30 for the year.",
  "apbio-u8-019": "Drought exposure does not depend on population density, whereas close contact at high density promotes transmission. A factor can reduce population size without being density dependent.",
  "apbio-u8-020": "Replicated invaded and uninvaded plots with other conditions controlled test whether invasion predicts reduced recruitment. Unmatched times or habitats and failure to count seedlings do not test the proposed response.",
  "apbio-u1-021": "At 55 degrees, the blue reference curve is near 0.30 and the orange variant curve is near 0.62. Reversing those values or reading another temperature does not match the plotted points.",
  "apbio-u1-022": "Above 35 degrees, the variant curve remains consistently higher, indicating better retention of activity. Neither enzyme gains activity as temperature rises.",
  "apbio-u1-023": "Because the variant retains a larger active fraction during heating, cells expressing it may preserve more enzyme function under moderate heat stress. The graph measures stability, not transcription or substrate identity.",
  "apbio-u1-024": "Extra ionic, hydrogen-bond, or hydrophobic interactions can raise thermal stability by resisting unfolding. Enzymes still require peptide bonds and amino-acid polymers.",
  "apbio-u2-021": "The 1-micrometer cube has the highest shown ratio, 6:1, and therefore the most membrane surface per unit volume. The ratio decreases as cube size rises.",
  "apbio-u2-022": "The displayed ratios fall from 6:1 to 3:1 to 1.5:1 as side length doubles. Volume grows faster than surface area, so their ratio cannot remain constant.",
  "apbio-u2-023": "The 4-micrometer cell has the lowest surface area per unit volume and therefore the least exchange capacity relative to metabolic demand. A high ratio delays rather than accelerates that limitation.",
  "apbio-u2-024": "Eight 2-micrometer cubes have the same combined volume as one 4-micrometer cube but more total exposed surface. Each daughter has the higher 3:1 ratio shown.",
  "apbio-u5-021": "The green exchange marker appears while replicated homologs are paired, before homolog separation in meiosis I. Crossing over does not occur after haploid products form.",
  "apbio-u5-022": "Crossing over occurs between nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, which contain corresponding loci. Sister chromatids begin as nearly identical copies and are not the modeled exchange partners.",
  "apbio-u5-023": "Reduction occurs when the two homologs are partitioned into different cells during meiosis I. Sister-chromatid separation in meiosis II preserves the already haploid chromosome count.",
  "apbio-u5-024": "Exchange places segments from maternal and paternal homologs into new combinations on individual chromatids. It changes linkage relationships without increasing chromosome number.",
  "apbio-u7-025": "At generation 8, the treated red curve is plotted near 0.64. The control remains near 0.08, and the treated curve reaches about 0.82 only at generation 10.",
  "apbio-u7-026": "The red treated curve rises steadily while the green control curve remains near its starting value. The populations therefore diverge rather than changing at the same rate.",
  "apbio-u7-027": "If R confers resistance, pesticide exposure gives R carriers greater survival and reproductive success, increasing R in the treated population. Selection changes frequencies; it does not direct allele conversion.",
  "apbio-u7-028": "If resistance is costly without pesticide, removing treatment reverses the selective advantage and R may decline. Allele-frequency trajectories can reverse when environmental selection changes.",
};

